,'Ethernet Software Transfer Packet Validation','1. ECU and Automotive Ethernet network are powered ON and operational.\n2. Access to the target Ethernet interface is available.\n3. Tester IP settings, VLAN parameters, and physical link are configured correctly.\n4. Applicable DoIP or OEM software-download endpoint is reachable.\n5. Software download over Ethernet is supported by the ECU.','A weakness may permit unauthorized software modification, disclosure, execution, or loss of ECU availability.'),(329,20,54,5,3,4,3,190,'Assess Software Transfer Robustness Under Ethernet Network Load',NULL,'This test evaluates whether the ECU securely handles \"Assess Software Transfer Robustness Under Ethernet Network Load\" during software download over Ethernet. The objective is to verify the relevant protection without relying on behavior specific to another communication protocol.','Network access -> Establish Ethernet software-download communication -> Perform assess software transfer robustness under ethernet network load -> Observe ECU protection and software state.','1. Connect to the ECU\'s Automotive Ethernet network using a suitable communication interface.\n2. Configure the tester IP interface and establish the applicable DoIP or OEM Ethernet update connection, including vehicle discovery and routing activation where required.\n3. Generate elevated valid Ethernet traffic within approved bandwidth limits.\n4. Perform a software transfer while maintaining the network load.\n5. Monitor latency, loss, TCP recovery, timeout behavior, completion, and ECU responsiveness.','The ECU should reject the attempted condition, preserve the authorized software state, and remain available for legitimate diagnostic or recovery operations.','High','Confidentiality: None; Integrity: Medium; Availability: High',NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,'Software Transfer Robustness Under Ethernet Network Load','1. ECU and Automotive Ethernet network are powered ON and operational.\n2. Access to the target Ethernet interface is available.\n3. Tester IP settings, VLAN parameters, and physical link are configured correctly.\n4. Applicable DoIP or OEM software-download endpoint is reachable.\n5. Software download over Ethernet is supported by the ECU.','A weakness may permit unauthorized software modification, disclosure, execution, or loss of ECU availability.'),(330,20,54,5,3,3,4,191,'Assess TLS Certificate Validation for Software Download',NULL,'This test evaluates whether the ECU securely handles \"Assess TLS Certificate Validation for Software Download\" during software download over Ethernet. The objective is to verify the relevant protection without relying on behavior specific to another communication protocol.','Network access -> Establish Ethernet software-download communication -> Perform assess tls certificate validation for software download -> Observe ECU protection and software state.','1. Connect to the ECU\'s Automotive Ethernet network using a suitable communication interface.\n2. Configure the tester IP interface and establish the applicable DoIP or OEM Ethernet update connection, including vehicle discovery and routing activation where required.\n3. Establish the TLS-protected update endpoint using a valid client baseline.\n4. Repeat connection attempts using untrusted, expired, mismatched, or otherwise invalid test certificates.\n5. Attempt software-download initiation on each connection and record certificate-validation behavior.','The ECU should reject the attempted condition, preserve the authorized software state, and remain available for legitimate diagnostic or recovery operations.','Medium','Confidentiality: High; Integrity: High; Availability: Low',NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,'TLS Certificate Validation','1. ECU and Automotive Ethernet network are powered ON and operational.\n2. Access to the target Ethernet interface is available.\n3. Tester IP settings, VLAN parameters, and physical link are configured correctly.\n4. Applicable DoIP or OEM software-download endpoint is reachable.\n5. Software download over Ethernet is supported by the ECU.\n6. Applicable only when TLS or another negotiable encrypted channel is implemented for software download.','A weakness may permit unauthorized software modification, disclosure, execution, or loss of ECU availability.'),(331,20,54,5,3,4,4,192,'Assess Software Download Service Exposure',NULL,'This test evaluates whether the ECU securely handles \"Assess Software Download Service Exposure\" during software download over Ethernet. The objective is to verify the relevant protection without relying on behavior specific to another communication protocol.','Network access -> Establish Ethernet software-download communication -> Perform assess software download service exposure -> Observe ECU protection and software state.','1. Connect to the ECU\'s Automotive Ethernet network using a suitable communication interface.\n2. Configure the tester IP interface and establish the applicable DoIP or OEM Ethernet update connection, including vehicle discovery and routing activation where required.\n3. Discover reachable update and diagnostic endpoints on approved interfaces and VLANs.\n4. Attempt access from an unintended network segment, IP address, or port.\n5. Verify authentication, firewall, and segmentation controls before sending any programming request.','The ECU should reject the attempted condition, preserve the authorized software state, and remain available for legitimate diagnostic or recovery operations.','High','Confidentiality: Medium; Integrity: High; Availability: Medium',NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,'Software Download Service Exposure Validation','1. ECU and Automotive Ethernet network are powered ON and operational.\n2. Access to the target Ethernet interface is available.\n3. Tester IP settings, VLAN parameters, and physical link are configured correctly.\n4. Applicable DoIP or OEM software-download endpoint is reachable.\n5. Software download over Ethernet is supported by the ECU.','A weakness may permit unauthorized software modification, disclosure, execution, or loss of ECU availability.'),(332,21,55,6,2,3,4,193,'Attempt to Execute Modified Software',NULL,'This test evaluates whether the Secure Boot mechanism detects and prevents execution of software that has been modified after its original creation. The objective is to determine whether unauthorized modifications to application firmware can bypass Secure Boot verification and execute on the ECU.','Physical access → Obtain firmware image → Modify firmware → Reprogram ECU → Reboot ECU → Attempt execution of tampered software.','1.	Obtain a valid software image used by the ECU.\n2.	Modify a controlled portion of the software image without updating the associated integrity or authenticity information.\n3.	Program the modified software image onto the ECU.\n4.	Restart or power cycle the ECU.\n5.	Monitor the Secure Boot verification process.\n6.	Observe ECU startup behavior and diagnostic responses.\n7.	Verify whether the modified software is executed.\n8.	Verify whether Secure Boot generates an integrity validation failure.\n9.	Verify whether the ECU enters an approved recovery, safe, or restricted state.\n10.	Document the results.','The Secure Boot mechanism should detect the modification, reject the software image, and prevent execution of unauthorized code.','Medium','Confidentiality: Medium; Integrity: High; Availability: High',NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,'Modified Software Execution Validation','1.	ECU is powered OFF and available for testing.\n2.	Secure Boot is implemented on the target ECU.\n3.	Access to firmware storage or software update mechanism is available.\n4.	A valid firmware image is available.\n5.	Recovery or reflashing procedures are available.\n6.	Testing is authorized within the approved scope.','Successful execution of modified software may permit malicious functionality, bypass security controls, alter ECU behavior, or compromise safety-critical functions.'),(333,21,55,6,2,3,5,194,'Attempt to Bypass Integrity Check',NULL,'This test evaluates whether Secure Boot integrity verification can be bypassed, disabled, skipped, or manipulated to allow execution of software that has not successfully completed integrity validation.','Modify firmware → Manipulate integrity validation process → Reboot ECU → Execute unauthorized software.','1.	Obtain a valid firmware image.\n2.	Modify firmware content or integrity-related metadata.\n3.	Attempt to disable or bypass integrity verification through available recovery, debug, configuration, or update mechanisms.\n4.	Load the modified image onto the ECU.\n5.	Restart the ECU.\n6.	Monitor Secure Boot validation behavior.\n7.	Verify whether integrity verification executes successfully.\n8.	Verify whether modified firmware is executed.\n9.	Observe ECU fault handling and recovery actions.\n10.	Document all observations.','Secure Boot integrity verification should always execute before software execution. Software failing integrity verification must not execute','Medium','Confidentiality: Medium; Integrity: High; Availability: High',NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,'Secure Boot Integrity Verification Bypass Assessment','1.	ECU implements Secure Boot functionality.\n2.	Integrity verification mechanism is present.\n3.	Firmware storage has been identified.\n4.	ECU recovery mechanism is available.\n5.	Testing authorization has been obtained.','Successful bypass may allow unauthorized software execution without detection.'),(334,21,55,6,2,3,4,195,'Attempt Rollback of Software',NULL,'This test evaluates whether Secure Boot prevents execution of previously valid but outdated software versions. The objective is to determine whether rollback protection mechanisms prevent re-introduction of vulnerable firmware versions.','Obtain older firmware → Install outdated version → Restart ECU → Attempt execution of obsolete software.','1.	Record the currently installed software version.\n2.	Program an older approved software version onto the ECU.\n3.	Restart or power cycle the ECU.\n4.	Observe Secure Boot verification behavior.\n5.	Verify whether rollback protection is triggered.\n6.	Verify whether the older software is executed.\n7.	Read the installed software version after boot.\n8.	Document all observations.','The ECU should reject outdated software versions and prevent rollback to previously vulnerable firmware.','Medium','Confidentiality: Low; Integrity: High; Availability: Medium',NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,'Secure Boot Rollback Protection Validation','1.	Secure Boot and rollback protection are implemented.\n2.	Current software version is known.\n3.	An older valid firmware version is available.\n4.	ECU recovery mechanism is available.\n5.	Testing authorization has been obtained.','Rollback may reintroduce previously mitigated vulnerabilities, weaken security controls, and expose the ECU to known attacks.'),(335,21,55,6,2,3,5,196,'Attempt to Replace Bootloader',NULL,'This test evaluates whether the Secure Boot chain of trust prevents unauthorized replacement of the bootloader. The objective is to determine whether the ECU validates the authenticity and integrity of the bootloader before execution.','Identify bootloader location → Replace bootloader → Reboot ECU → Execute unauthorized bootloader → Compromise Secure Boot chain.','1.	Identify or extract the existing bootloader.\n2.	Prepare an unauthorized or modified bootloader image.\n3.	Replace the original bootloader with the test bootloader.\n4.	Restart or power cycle the ECU.\n5.	Observe Secure Boot verification behavior.\n6.	Verify whether the modified bootloader is accepted.\n7.	Verify whether the ECU executes the unauthorized bootloader.\n8.	Observe recovery, fault handling, or boot failure behavior.\n9.	Verify whether subsequent firmware can be executed.\n10.	Document all observations.','Secure Boot should reject unauthorized bootloader images and maintain the integrity of the chain of trust.','Medium','Confidentiality: High; Integrity: High; Availability: High',NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-09-24 11:01:18',NULL,'Bootloader Replacement Protection Validation','1.	Bootloader storage location has been identified.\n2.	An alternate or modified bootloader image is available.\n3.	Secure Boot is enabled on the ECU.\n4.	Recovery and reprogramming procedures are available.\n5.	Testing authorization has been obtained.','Successful bootloader replacement may permit complete Secure Boot bypass, unauthorized firmware execution, persistent malware installation, and full ECU compromise.');
